		{"id":"fc4a5fefbc6532d7","type":"text","text":"you can now see the item in your inventory","x":340,"y":-460,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"1214dd224f6d4bf0","type":"text","text":"if you use the item text shows \"you used *item name*\"","x":340,"y":-360,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"24c348f258ebdd06","type":"text","text":"if its a one time use item text shows \"*item name* has been destroyed\"","x":340,"y":-260,"width":250,"height":101},
		{"id":"090d6ec95e90154b","type":"text","text":"If you are using Nathan Hoad’s popular **Godot Dialogue Manager** plugin, the absolute best way to handle inventory checks is by ==leveraging **Global Autoloads (Singletons)**==. Because Godot Dialogue Manager seamlessly evaluates real-time GDScript variables and methods exposed globally, you can query your inventory directly from your dialogue files (`.dialogue`) without duplicating state data.\n\nHere are the **three best ways** to implement inventory-based dialogue checks using this workflow:\n\nSetup Strategy: The Inventory Autoload\n\nBefore looking at the approaches, ensure you have an Autoload script named **`Inventory`** registered in your Project Settings.\n\ngdscript\n\n```\n# inventory.gd (Registered as an Autoload named 'Inventory')\nextends Node\n\n# Stores item_id: quantity\nvar items: Dictionary = {\n\t\"rusty_key\": 1,\n\t\"gold_coin\": 15\n}\n\nfunc has_item(item_id: String, amount: int = 1) -> bool:\n\treturn items.get(item_id, 0) >= amount\n\nfunc remove_item(item_id: String, amount: int = 1) -> void:\n\tif has_item(item_id, amount):\n\t\titems[item_id] -= amount\n```\n\nUse code with caution.\n\nIn your Godot Dialogue Manager settings, ensure **`Inventory`** is added to the list of \"Globals\" so your `.dialogue` files can read it.\n\nWay 1: The Conditional Logic Approach (Best for Branching Conversations)\n\nThis is the native, cleanest way to show or hide lines based on what the player is holding. You use GDScript conditional formatting directly inside your dialogue script.\n\n**Your `.dialogue` File:**\n\ndialogue\n\n```\n~ start\nGuard: Halt! No one enters the castle without a pass.\n- Bribe him with gold [if Inventory.has_item(\"gold_coin\", 10)]\n    do Inventory.remove_item(\"gold_coin\", 10)\n    Guard: Welcome inside, traveler. Keep it quiet.\n    => castle_entrance\n- Try to talk your way in\n    Guard: Not going to happen. Scram.\n    => start\n- Leave\n    => END\n```\n\nUse code with caution.\n\n- **Pros:** Highly readable; completely handles choice pruning dynamically.\n- **Cons:** Hardcoded item ID strings inside text files can become brittle if you change item names later.\n\nWay 2: The Direct Custom Method / Script Mutation Call (Best for Item Hand-offs)\n\nIf your dialogue needs to trigger a complex inventory event (like checking a condition, removing an item, and automatically adding a reward item all at once), you can encapsulate this logic inside a method on your Autoload and call it via the `do` statement.\n\n**GDScript (`inventory.gd` extension):**\n\ngdscript\n\n```\nfunc try_give_key_to_npc() -> bool:\n\tif has_item(\"rusty_key\"):\n\t\tremove_item(\"rusty_key\")\n\t\t# Maybe trigger an achievement or quest update here\n\t\treturn true\n\treturn false\n```\n\nUse code with caution.\n\n**Your `.dialogue` File:**\n\ndialogue\n\n```\n~ old_man_chat\nOld Man: Did you find my old locker key in the dungeon?\n- Yes, here it is.\n    if Inventory.try_give_key_to_npc()\n        Old Man: Oh, thank goodness! Here is your reward.\n        do Inventory.add_item(\"potion\", 1)\n    else\n        Old Man: Don't lie to an old man, you don't have it.\n- Not yet.\n    Old Man: Please hurry back if you find it.\n```\n\nUse code with caution.\n\n- **Pros:** Keeps heavy logic out of dialogue files and safe inside structured GDScript code.\n- **Cons:** Splits the logic; you have to bounce between the dialogue file and your script to see what `try_give_key_to_npc()` actually checks.\n\nWay 3: The Intercepted UI Presentation (Best for RPG-style \"Select Item\" Screens)\n\nIf you want the player to click a dialogue choice like _\"Give an item...\"_ which then pops up your game's **actual visual grid inventory UI** for them to choose from, you must bridge the dialogue system with your UI code using asynchronous tracking or signals.\n\n**Your `.dialogue` File:**\n\ndialogue\n\n```\n~ merchant\nMerchant: Show me something rare, and I'll trade with you.\n- Open inventory to show an item...\n    # Set a global state flag telling your game to wait for an item\n    set DialogueState.waiting_for_item_selection = true\n    # Emit a custom signal from a global tracker to open the UI panel\n    do DialogueState.emit_signal(\"request_item_select_ui\")\n    # Tell Godot Dialogue Manager to pause processing until the UI closes\n    => wait_for_ui\n\n~ wait_for_ui\n# The UI will resume the dialogue manager and point it to the correct branch\n```\n\nUse code with caution.\n\n**GDScript (`dialogue_state.gd` Autoload):**\n\ngdscript\n\n```\nextends Node\nsignal request_item_select_ui\nvar selected_item_id: String = \"\"\nvar waiting_for_item_selection: bool = false\n```\n\nUse code with caution.\n\n**Your Visual Inventory UI Script:**\n\ngdscript\n\n```\n# Connected to the item slot click event while the dialogue is open\nfunc _on_item_slot_clicked(item_id: String):\n\tif DialogueState.waiting_for_item_selection:\n\t\tDialogueState.selected_item_id = item_id\n\t\tDialogueState.waiting_for_item_selection = false\n\t\t\n\t\t# Close the inventory UI\n\t\thide() \n\t\t\n\t\t# Determine where dialogue should go next based on what they picked\n\t\tvar next_line = \"merchant_accepts\" if item_id == \"dragon_egg\" else \"merchant_rejects\"\n\t\t\n\t\t# Resume the dialogue tree manually using the plugin's resource engine\n\t\tDialogueManager.show_dialogue_balloon(load(\"res://dialogue/main.dialogue\"), next_line)\n```\n\nUse code with caution.\n\n- **Pros:** Provides a premium, high-production RPG feel where the player interacts with a proper grid UI rather than just reading lists of text options.\n- **Cons:** Complex to engineer; requires careful management of game states, UI visibility, and pausing dialogue progression safely.\n\nWhich of these matches the scope of your project? If you want to dive deeper, let me know:\n\n- Are your items defined as **Resource files (`.tres`)** or simple **Strings/IDs**?\n- Do you need help setting up the **signal plumbing** for the visual inventory pop-up (Way 3)?","x":1920,"y":-1200,"width":1440,"height":1640},
		{"id":"34b0dcf9ee13c0a5","type":"text","text":"how to implement inventory godot (AI!!!!)","x":2520,"y":-1360,"width":250,"height":80},
